   }
  ],
  "Vectors_Act1_WhatAVectorIs": [
   {
    "id": "note-1",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "The only room in the chapter whose floor already matches its lesson one to one — a where, a which-way, a how-far and a how-far refused, and deliberately no arithmetic; leave the four stations exactly as they stand."
   }
  ],
  "VFM_02_Operations": [
   {
    "id": "note-1",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "The bench. Twenty-seven instruments and not one caption: every operation here is said again in the captioned room next door, at walk-in scale."
   }
  ],
  "Vectors_Act2_VectorArithmetic": [
   {
    "id": "note-1",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "The tutorial. Eight caption panels down column 9 and one rung beside each: add, subtract, scale, dot, project, cross, and the two laser exhibits at the door."
   },
   {
    "id": "note-2",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "The tutorial hall for rung 2, and the room where the chapter's two-scale rhythm is said inside a single space: six operations, each once in your hand and once at the size of a room, in the order the eight caption panels stand down the centre aisle. Ten rungs on the thread, hand then room at each of the four paired stations; the two laser exhibits at the door stay in the room as a third scale met after the closing line, not as a lesson. The bench next door holds the same instruments and defers all of them here, so this is where an operation is actually taught."
   }
  ],
  "VFM_05_Launch": [
   {
    "id": "note-1",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "Thirty-one placements and the room says throw sixteen ways, so the tutorial is F=ma, then the net force, then the arc composed and decomposed, then you as the projectile, then many bodies, then the census that shows all four launchers running one equation; friction_ramp is the one artifact here that would be better MOVED than demoted — it belongs beside wedge_slide in Act4a."
   }
  ],
  "Vectors_Act3_FieldsAndMotion": [
   {
    "id": "note-1",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "Six of this hall's artifacts say objective 9 on one entry shelf, so the rule and the walk-in carry it as a pair and the shelf becomes the gallery of other ways to draw a field; I place bubble_blaster third rather than last, against final.md's running order, because on the floor it stands north of VectorMotion and closing on it would send the visitor back — worth Palle's ruling."
   },
   {
    "id": "note-2",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "Two halves that turn out to be one thing: a field is force spread over space, motion is force spread over time. The field half opens on the rule and closes by putting you inside it; the motion half opens on the a→v→p chain, decomposes a launch, and closes on the bubble gun, where a single motion and a whole field are shown to be the same object seen at two counts. The eight-piece entry shelf beside the spawn is a gallery of other ways to draw a field — it stays in the room and out of the text."
   }
  ],
  "Vectors_Act4a_KineticForces": [
   {
    "id": "note-1",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "The park is already a clean five-rung tutorial — work, drag, turn, fall, slope — and the only change is force_pad, which keeps its floor beat and its place at the park's midpoint but stops carrying a lesson launch already owns; the two category heroes stay exactly where they are, doing a hero's job of setting the register before the beats speak."
   }
  ],
  "Vectors_Act4b_Oscillation": [
   {
    "id": "note-1",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "FLAG for Palle: the room's law is F = -kx and the floor delivers spring_tower LAST — spawn is south, the tower is north — so the room's own tutorial.md opens on the artifact the walk closes on; swapping spawn and teleporter is the one map change that would make this room's order correct, and it is a decision for you, not for this pass."
   }
  ],
  "VFM_07_Gravity": [
   {
    "id": "note-1",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "Two placement flags outrank the roles here: three_body_problem, the hardest idea in the chapter, stands four cells from the spawn and is met before the law it breaks, and chaos_attractor stands one row SOUTH of the exit teleporter, so a visitor walking to the door reaches the door first."
   },
   {
    "id": "note-2",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "One law taken from two bodies to three, until the arithmetic stops being able to tell you the future, and then handed to a graph that lays itself out. The room is a bench, not a walk — its six primaries are the six sections of its own wall text, one headline each, and its five secondaries say those same six things a second way (the barycentre already met in the park, the clamp, the count without the cost, the layout on a grid, and Lorenz standing past the exit)."
   }
  ],
  "VFM_09_Legs": [
   {
    "id": "note-1",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "The one room that needs no rearranging — a controlled series with everything held equal but the count, which only reads against itself, so it should show as ONE card; it also exposes a real gap, because none of the seventeen objectives covers static stability or gait, and this room is good enough to deserve an eighteenth."
   }
  ],
  "VFM_08_Arena": [
   {
    "id": "note-1",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "The exam: this hall introduces no force, so its four primaries are the four places an already-taught vector has to be AIMED at something under time pressure, and its whole north prop row is the chapter restated as furniture — good gallery, not a rung; tutorial.md still opens the hall on the cradle, which is the chapter's third cradle, and that alone is why the room reads as having no order."
   }
  ],
  "Vectors_Act5_ForceAsPlace": [
   {
    "id": "note-1",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "A clean four-move loop already — state the premise at the door, dial the field, throw a mass in to read it, step in yourself — with one honest gap the map's own field_notes record: columns 6 and 7 are solid floor across the chasm, so a visitor can reach the far side without ever setting the field, and the closing lesson is primary but not yet compelled."
   }
  ],
  "VFM_03_Motion": [
   {
    "id": "note-1",
    "role": "primary",
    "text": "The motion bench. Its own intent names the stations: the eight-metre bouncing-ball chamber anchors it, the friction threshold and the normal force stand together, and the collision crasher prowls the far corner behind a warning."
   }
  ]
 },
